From b406e26ce6ad2db36ffc392024e6afcf84ab46d2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 17:07:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(graphql): bind updateIncidentStatus to the input type its
 schema declares
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

updateIncidentStatus declares IncidentStatusInput! in incident.graphql but its
resolver binds UpdateIncidentStatusInput, a second input type with an identical
field set. bindArgument deserializes the raw argument map into whichever class
it is handed, so the mismatch is invisible at runtime — today. It stops being
invisible the moment either type gains a field the other lacks: the mutation
would silently drop it, with no compile error and no schema validation error,
because each half remains independently valid.

The rest of the codebase already agrees on IncidentStatusInput. It is the
mutation's declared argument type, the type of RaiseIncidentInput.status and
UpdateIncidentInput.status, the parameter of IncidentUtils.mapIncidentStatus,
and the type used by three resolver tests. UpdateIncidentStatusInput was
referenced from exactly one file, the resolver changed here.

The resolver now binds IncidentStatusInput, matching the schema and its sibling
mapIncidentStatus, which performs the same state/stage/message mapping. The
now-genuinely-unreferenced type is removed.

This direction was chosen deliberately. The schema is the wire contract, so
clients already have to send IncidentStatusInput; aligning the resolver to the
schema is invisible to every working client, whereas changing the schema to
UpdateIncidentStatusInput would break any client naming the type in a variable
definition. Ordering matters within the change: graphql-java-codegen generates
a class for every declared type and the output is built rather than committed,
so deleting the schema type before switching the resolver would break
compileJava.

Also corrected in docs/incidents/incidents.md, which disagreed with the schema
in three ways: it documented the argument as UpdateIncidentStatusInput!, the
return type as String where the schema says Boolean, and omitted the stage
field that the type carries and the resolver reads. A user following it wrote a
mutation the server rejects, naming a type the documentation told them to use.

One typo in the docstring of the surviving type: "stage ofthe incident". It was
the only such instance in the file.
